---
const stats = [
  { n: 3.82, d: 2, suffix: '', label: 'diploma GPA, Director’s Honour Roll' },
  { n: 10, d: 0, suffix: ' mo', label: 'full-stack internship at A*STAR' },
  { n: 640, d: 0, suffix: '+', label: 'tests guarding my latest game' },
  { n: 5, d: 0, suffix: '+', label: 'projects deploying themselves through CI' },
];
---

<section id="about">
  <div class="container grid">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow" data-reveal>01 / about</p>
      <h2 class="h2" data-reveal data-scramble-on-view>Engineer by habit, <span class="grad">curious by default.</span></h2>
    </div>
    <div class="body">
      <p data-reveal style="--i:1">
        I learned to build software by hand. At Singapore Polytechnic, where I made the Director’s Honour Roll, my team and I
        built a Java student management system, a React dungeon crawler and a restaurant ordering menu, and worked through AWS
        serverless, secure coding and MySQL projects. I then spent ten months at <strong>A*STAR</strong> shipping Angular
        screens and .NET APIs for a last-mile logistics platform.
      </p>
      <p data-reveal style="--i:2">
        Today I study <strong>Computer Science at NUS</strong> and work as a <strong>software engineer</strong> on my own
        projects and a student team. I'm aiming at <strong>DevOps and cloud</strong>: every project here builds, tests and
        deploys itself through CI, and I want to go further into containers, infrastructure and reliability.
      </p>
      <p data-reveal style="--i:3">
        I'm <strong>eager to learn whatever is new</strong>, and right now that's what AI can really do. I use coding agents
        the way I'd use any powerful tool: I still design the system, write and review code, and check every change with tests.
        The fastest way I learn something is to build a real thing with it, so some experiments are below.
      </p>
      <p data-reveal style="--i:4">
        I care about software that is <strong>tested, honest about what it does, and safe for the people using it</strong>,
        which is why I am drawn to public-facing services, cloud platforms and security.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="container">
    <ul class="stats">
      {stats.map((s, i) => (
        <li data-reveal style={`--i:${i}`}>
          <span class="num" data-count={s.n} data-decimals={s.d} data-suffix={s.suffix}>{s.n.toFixed(s.d)}{s.suffix}</span>
          <span class="lbl">{s.label}</span>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1.1fr;
    gap: clamp(28px, 6vw, 90px);
    align-items: start;
  }
  .body p {
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: clamp(17px, 1.5vw, 19px);
    margin: 0 0 20px;
  }
  .body strong {
    color: var(--text);
    font-weight: 600;
  }
  .stats {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 64px 0 0;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
  }
  .stats li {
    padding: 28px 20px 0 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 8px;
  }
  .stats li + li {
    padding-left: 20px;
    border-left: 1px solid var(--line);
  }
  .num {
    font-size: clamp(36px, 4.6vw, 56px);
    font-weight: 650;
    letter-spacing: -0.04em;
    line-height: 1;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }
  .lbl {
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 14px;
    max-width: 22ch;
  }
  @media (max-width: 860px) {
    .grid {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
    .stats {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    }
    .stats li:nth-child(3) {
      border-left: 0;
      padding-left: 0;
    }
    .stats li {
      padding-bottom: 20px;
    }
  }
</style>
